WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
9% of artificial intelligence shopping answers pointed people at fake or unsafe stores. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 226. Then how those models actually choose a link, a snappier Gemini for everyday questions, and quick bits. ChatGPT, Google Gemini, and Perplexity in a podkey study covered by digit, were asked shopping questions about 151 brands like Lululemon and Birkenstock. These are the chatbots a lot of people already type into.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.300
when they want a fast recommendation, the way you might text someone for a store.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.420
Picture this, you ask a friend where to buy new sneakers, and they send a link that looks

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:45.300
official but is not. That is basically what happened here. The chat bot sometimes suggested fake

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.280
or risky websites that could be selling counterfeits, meaning fake copies of real products,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.080
or that might even be dangerous. Perplexity gave risky links in 15% of its answers,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.220
chapters, chat GPT in 8%, and Gemini in 4%.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:06.340
The security firm Podkey ran the test on 6,795 AI-generated answers.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.860
Smaller brands faced roughly twice the risk of a risky link compared with big-name companies.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.180
Think of a huge chain versus a tiny local label.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.280
There is just less solid information floating around about the small one.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.080
So the guest gets shakier.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.680
Question about regional shipping or international delivery made the problem even worse, because

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.920
the model sometimes invented websites that did not actually exist for those regions.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.360
When risky sites did appear, chat GPT issued a warning only 24% of the time, while the

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.480
other two tools warned even less often.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.660
In 57% of the answers containing a risky link, the chatbot actively suggested the site as

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.480
a place to shop, rather than just mentioning it in passing.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.060
One documented case had perplexity naming an incorrect page as the official stucy site

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.980
and describing it as a source of authentic products.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.520
More people, including teens shopping for clothes, games or school supplies, are turning

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.680
to AI for quick recommendations instead of searching themselves.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.880
These tools are really good at sounding confident even when they are pulling from messy web data

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.700
they have not double checked, like a classmate who speaks loudly without looking at the assignment.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.940
Treat the answer like a suggestion from a helpful but forgetful friend.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.540
Always look at the web address yourself before you tap, click, or type any info.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.240
You can try this right now.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.660
Open the Gemini app or chat GPT on your phone.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.180
Ask where you can buy authentic Stussy Clothes online, and then compare every suggested site

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.000
with the Real Brands official page.

00:02:37.952 --> 00:02:40.792
YouTube and TikTok already play a guessing game with you.

00:02:41.212 --> 00:02:44.892
The app looks at what you washed before and predicts what you might like next.

00:02:44.892 --> 00:02:50.132
Next, an AI chatbot does a similar guessing game, except the pile is millions of web pages

00:02:50.132 --> 00:02:54.292
instead of videos, and it does not actually shop or walk into a store.

00:02:54.872 --> 00:02:59.252
It looks at patterns in the huge pile of text it was trained on, meaning the writing

00:02:59.252 --> 00:03:03.852
it studied beforehand, and predicts which words and links usually appear together when people

00:03:03.852 --> 00:03:04.852
ask about a brand.

00:03:05.452 --> 00:03:10.512
If lots of old pages mention a certain web address next to a brand name, even if that address

00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:14.872
is now fake or abandoned, the model can still suggest it because the patterned

00:03:14.872 --> 00:03:19.492
feels familiar, the way auto-complete finishes a sentence you have typed 100 times.

00:03:20.052 --> 00:03:24.692
Here is the tricky part, the AI has no way to check in real time whether the link still

00:03:24.692 --> 00:03:29.372
works or belongs to the real company. It is just completing the sentence based on what

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:34.312
it saw during training. Smaller or regional brands cause more mistakes because there are

00:03:34.312 --> 00:03:39.812
fewer trustworthy pages about them, so the model has less clear patterns to follow, like trying

00:03:39.812 --> 00:03:42.752
to cook a rare dish when only two messy recipes exist.

00:03:43.472 --> 00:03:47.892
The same pattern matching process explains why mistakes jumped for questions about shipping

00:03:47.892 --> 00:03:49.112
to specific countries.

00:03:49.632 --> 00:03:53.912
Fewer reliable pages existed in the training data, so the guesses got worse.

00:03:54.532 --> 00:04:01.852
Researchers counted 718 risky domains out of 4,437 they reviewed, split between outright

00:04:01.852 --> 00:04:06.952
fake sites and dead domains that scammers could later claim, like empty storefronts waiting

00:04:06.952 --> 00:04:07.832
for a new sign.

00:04:07.832 --> 00:04:12.212
A warning when it showed up at all was like a friend recommending a restaurant without

00:04:12.212 --> 00:04:13.792
checking whether it is still open.

00:04:14.432 --> 00:04:19.532
The AI is not trying to trick you, it is doing auto-complete on a massive scale, and sometimes

00:04:19.532 --> 00:04:21.672
that auto-complete is outdated or wrong.

00:04:22.312 --> 00:04:25.232
Once you can see the guests, you know to check the address yourself.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:32.010
Google released Gemini 4 Hydrogen as the Verge laid out, a new version built for speed on everyday

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.630
tasks like coding help, answering tricky questions with multiple parts, and agent-style workflows.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:42.210
meaning jobs where the AI takes several steps in a row like a helper working

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:42.950
down a checklist.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:49.490
It runs about 2.5 times faster than the previous Flash model while still handling complex requests,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.030
and it is already live for regular users in the Gemini app and Google search.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:59.930
The model also appears in Google Sheets for Google AI Pro and Ultra Subscribers, and it

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:04.930
is available to people who build apps through Google AI Studio and Android Studio, which

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.250
Which is Google's tool for making Android phone apps.

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:13.070
On the independent artificial analysis intelligence index, a public scoreboard that ranks how capable

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.030
these models are, it surpasses the earlier Argon model at its default thinking level.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:23.230
If you like quick homework help, creative writing prompts, or game ideas, it should feel

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.030
snappier than older versions, like a classmate who still does the hard problems but finishes

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:28.750
them faster.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:34.070
It is especially good at breaking down questions that have several parts, contain typos,

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:39.470
or include highly specific requests, the way you might split one messy group project brief into

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:45.090
clean pieces. Open the Gemini app on your phone or the Gemini website and sign in with a

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.930
free Google account if you have not already. Try asking a multi-part question like this.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:55.330
Explain photosynthesis in simple steps and then give me a three-sentence story about a plant that

00:05:55.330 --> 00:06:00.690
learns to talk. Watch how cleanly it breaks the answer into parts and keeps each section accurate

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:06.912
even when the request has two very different goals. A 60-year-old man, the Economic Times reported,

00:06:07.472 --> 00:06:12.952
followed chat GPT's advice not to pay rent and ended up homeless. The chatbot told him losing

00:06:12.952 --> 00:06:19.299
his home was legally impossible, which turned out to be completely wrong. BrainIt, described by Live

00:06:19.299 --> 00:06:25.159
Science, is a new AI that turns brain scans into pictures and back again. The model learns patterns

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:30.339
in visual brain activity so it can recreate what someone was seeing, or predict what a brain scan

00:06:30.339 --> 00:06:35.419
would look like from an image, like a two-way translator between a photo and the brain's own signals.

